course: biology
lesson: 150
title: Myth check: do lemmings jump off cliffs?
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Many people believe lemmings follow each other off cliffs into the sea, and call anyone who follows the crowd blindly a «lemming». It is not true.
explain: The story became famous through a 1958 nature film: the film makers brought lemmings in and pushed them off a cliff for the camera, because they believed an old story and wanted a dramatic scene.
explain: The truth is stranger: lemming numbers boom and crash every three to four years, and in boom years crowds of lemmings spread out looking for food, swim rivers and lakes, and some drown, not because they want to die but because they are moving on.
explain: Picture the Arctic in winter: under the snow, in a hidden world near the ground, lemmings dig tunnels, build grass nests and even raise babies, while above them a snowy owl and an Arctic fox wait for one to pop up.
fact: Lemmings do not jump off cliffs on purpose; the famous 1958 film scene was staged by bringing lemmings in and pushing them off.
fact: Lemmings are small rodents related to voles, living on the tundra and mountains of Scandinavia, Russia, Canada and Alaska, eating grasses, moss and sedges.
fact: Lemming numbers rise and fall in cycles, often every three to four years: plenty of food means fast breeding, then food runs short, predators increase and numbers crash.
fact: In boom years lemmings spread out in search of food and may swim rivers and lakes, and some drown; this may be where the cliff myth began.
fact: Norwegian lemmings are bold and brightly coloured, and they often squeak and stand their ground against much bigger animals.
fact: Snowy owls, Arctic foxes, stoats and skuas depend on lemmings; in good years they raise big families, and in bad years snowy owls may not nest at all.
fact: An Arctic fox mother can have over ten cubs in a good lemming year.
fact: The 1991 computer game «Lemmings» made the myth more famous, though the player's job was to save them.
fact: An old Scandinavian folk story said lemmings fell from the sky in storms, because they appeared so suddenly in boom years.
fact: In winter lemmings live in a space between the ground and the snow; snow is full of trapped air, so it works like a blanket and keeps them near freezing point while the air above is far colder.
fact: Rain falling on snow can freeze into hard ice layers that seal lemmings off from their food and soak their nests, and in parts of Norway boom years became rarer for a while as winters changed.
fact: Some books and quizzes still repeat the cliff myth, because people copy facts without checking where they came from.
exercise: Do lemmings jump off cliffs on purpose? | No, that is a myth | Yes, every few years | Yes, when they are old | Only when they follow a leader
exercise: Where did the famous film of lemmings falling into the sea come from? | A 1958 film where lemmings were pushed off for the camera | A real wild migration in Norway that was filmed by chance from a boat | A cartoon that was mistaken for real | A photo taken by a scientist in Norway
exercise: What kind of animal is a lemming? | A small rodent related to voles | A small bird of the Arctic | A young Arctic fox | A kind of large insect
exercise: Where do lemmings live? | On tundra and mountains of the far north | In the hot, sandy deserts of North Africa | In the steamy rainforests of South America, high up in the trees | On small, warm islands close to the equator
exercise: Why do lemmings seem to be everywhere in some years? | Their numbers boom and crash every few years | They all come out of hiding at once after a long sleep in the ground | They fall from the sky in great storms, like rain | Farmers bring them in by the truckload each spring
exercise: Why do lemming numbers crash after a boom? | Food runs short and predators increase | They all jump into the sea | They all fly south for the winter | They stop having babies on purpose
exercise: Why do some lemmings drown? | They swim rivers and lakes while looking for food | They want to die when there are too many | They follow a leader to the coast and into the sea to drown themselves | They cannot swim at all
exercise: What does a Norwegian lemming often do when a person comes close? | Squeaks and stands its ground | Runs to the nearest cliff | Rolls over and plays dead | Climbs the nearest tree
exercise: Which animal depends on lemmings for food? | The snowy owl | The polar bear only | The Arctic walrus | The grazing reindeer
exercise: What did the 1991 computer game «Lemmings» ask players to do? | Save the lemmings from walking off ledges | Push as many lemmings off cliffs as possible | Count the lemmings as they fall off the cliff | Feed the lemmings to the hungry snowy owls
exercise: Where do lemmings live in winter? | In tunnels and nests between the ground and the snow | Far south where it is warm | High in trees above the snow | Deep in the sea under the thick ice, where the water stays warmer
exercise: Why is it warmer under the snow? | Snow is full of trapped air and works like a blanket | The lemmings light small fires | The ground in the Arctic is very hot underneath, like a heated floor | The snow melts and makes warm water
exercise: What happens to Arctic foxes in a year with lots of lemmings? | They can raise very big litters | They have no cubs at all | They move to the cities | They eat only berries
exercise: How can rain in winter harm lemmings? | It freezes into ice that seals them off from food | It washes them into the sea | It makes the grass grow too fast | It gives them colds and fevers, so many of them die in the spring
exercise: Why do some library books still say lemmings jump into the sea? | People copied the story without checking where it came from | Scientists found new proof last year | The books are really about a different animal with a similar name | Lemmings did it only in the past
exercise: What did an old Scandinavian folk story say about lemmings? | That they fell from the sky in storms | That they lived inside the mountains | That they were baby polar bears | That they could fly like birds
test: «lemmings are dumb, they just follow each other off cliffs, everyone knows that» What is right? | It is a myth; a 1958 film staged it by pushing lemmings off | Yes, scientists film it every year | Yes, only the leader decides to jump | No, they jump into rivers to drown, not off cliffs into the sea
test: In the old game «Lemmings», little lemmings walk off ledges one after another. Why is the game not proof? | Games repeat ideas people already believe, and the player's job was to save them | Games are always made from real nature films, so whatever they show must be true | The game was made by lemming scientists | The game showed the real numbers of lemmings
test: «4 lemmings and 1 stoat in the burrow, wait no, 5 lemmings, how many legs» | 24 | 20 | 6 | 28
test: What would happen to snowy owls in a year when lemming numbers crash? | Many would raise few young or not nest at all | They would raise bigger families than ever, with more babies in each nest | They would start eating grass | Nothing at all would change for them
test: «snow's freezing, how come lemmings don't freeze to death all winter» How do they survive? | Snow traps air like a blanket, keeping the space under it near freezing point, not far colder | Lemmings sleep frozen solid inside the ice all winter without needing to breathe at all | Lemmings swim south in the autumn | Lemmings grow thick shells in winter
test: A warm spell brings rain, then a hard frost turns the snow into an icy crust. What happens to lemmings under it? | They can be cut off from food and their nests get soaked | They are warmer and safer than before, as the ice works like a hard roof | They get more food than usual | Nothing, ice does not reach them
test: «lemmings swimming across the river, they're just chucking themselves in» What is right? | They are moving to find food and space, though some drown | Yes, they swim in to drown on purpose | Yes, lemmings hate their crowded lives in boom years and give up on purpose | No, lemmings never go near water
test: Why would an Arctic fox mother have over ten cubs one year and only a few the next? | Lemming numbers rose, then crashed | Foxes just choose a number at random | It got warmer, so more cubs fit in the den | The cubs from last year all came back
test: A person who copies whatever the crowd does is called «a lemming». Is that fair to real lemmings? | No, real lemmings do not follow each other off cliffs | Yes, lemmings copy every move of the leader | Yes, it was proven in a nature film | No, because lemmings never live in groups and always keep far apart from each other
test: «a squeaky lemming just stood up and squared up to me, they're cowards though?» What is right? | Norwegian lemmings are bold and often stand their ground | Yes, lemmings always run and hide | Yes, it was probably ill | No, lemmings cannot make any sound
test: In a cartoon a lemming falls out of a storm cloud. Where might that idea come from? | An old folk story, because lemmings appear so suddenly in boom years | Lemmings really live in clouds | Lemmings are carried up into the clouds by big birds and dropped again in storms | Scientists saw it happen in a lab
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the alleged leaping of lemmings from cliffs. | The notion is a myth, popularised by a film of 1958 in which the scene was contrived | The behaviour is regularly recorded by scientists in all years and in every region where the animal lives | The behaviour is confined to the eldest members of a group | The behaviour occurs exclusively in the Scandinavian winter
exam: Identify the principal cause of the periodic collapse in the numbers of lemmings. | The exhaustion of the food supply together with an increase in predators | A deliberate mass descent into the sea | Migration southward to temperate regions | A voluntary cessation of breeding
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the winter habitat of the lemming? | It dwells in a space between the ground and the snow, the snow serving to insulate it | It migrates in great numbers to warmer latitudes for the duration of the winter and returns in spring | It dwells within the branches of trees above the snow | It hibernates within the sea beneath the ice
exam: Ascertain the effect upon lemmings of rain falling upon snow and subsequently freezing. | Layers of ice may sever access to food and saturate the nests | The ice affords additional warmth and safety | The ice increases the quantity of food available | The ice exerts no effect whatever upon the animals, which remain secure within the soil
